From 4fca2f089b904bb9a359cec37b0270899b92642d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yusef Karim <yusefkarim@riseup.net>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 22:25:08 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] cts: guard against a null downstream net in
 separateMacroRegSinks

TritonCTS's characterization phase (configure_cts_characterization)
places dummy load instances (named by CtsOptions::getDummyLoadPrefix(),
"clkload" by default) on real, already-populated clock nets to
measure buffer/inverter delay and slew under representative fanout.
Those dummy instances' output pins are deliberately left unconnected,
since they exist to be loads, not drivers.

separateMacroRegSinks(), when later re-partitioning a net's sinks into
macro and register buckets, walks every ITerm on the net and, for any
whose master Liberty-qualifies as an inverter, unconditionally follows
inst->getFirstOutput()->getNet()->get1stSignalInput(false) to decide
whether the inverter drives a macro downstream. This assumes every
inverter found on a clock net is a real pass-through stage with a
valid downstream net. It has no exclusion for characterization-only
instances and no null check on the result, so when the net being
processed happens to also carry one of these dummy loads,
getFirstOutput()->getNet() returns nullptr and the call segfaults.

The crash is intermittent (roughly 1 in 100 runs on the design that
found this) because it only fires when two independently-nondeterministic
selections happen to coincide: which nets configure_cts_characterization
picks to attach a dummy load to, and which nets separateMacroRegSinks
later happens to reprocess. Both depend on OpenDB's unordered-container
iteration order.

Guard the output net and the result of get1stSignalInput() before
dereferencing either. When the inverter's output is unconnected (the
dummy-load case) or its net has no signal input, leave nonSinkMacro as
already computed from isSink() rather than following a null pointer.

Confirmed against a live coredump (rdi == 0x0 at the fault, i.e. the
dbNet* receiver of get1stSignalInput was null) and against the design's
own CTS checkpoint database, where all characterization dummy loads
with a floating output were found wired by their input pin onto real,
shared clock nets alongside genuine register clock pins.
---
 src/cts/src/TritonCTS.cpp | 19 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/cts/src/TritonCTS.cpp b/src/cts/src/TritonCTS.cpp
index 75bbd80..13c1dc0 100644
--- a/src/cts/src/TritonCTS.cpp
+++ b/src/cts/src/TritonCTS.cpp
@@ -1495,9 +1495,22 @@ bool TritonCTS::separateMacroRegSinks(
       sta::Cell* masterCell = network_->dbToSta(mterm->getMaster());
       sta::LibertyCell* libertyCell = network_->libertyCell(masterCell);
       if (libertyCell && libertyCell->isInverter()) {
-        odb::dbITerm* invertedTerm
-            = inst->getFirstOutput()->getNet()->get1stSignalInput(false);
-        nonSinkMacro &= invertedTerm->getInst()->isBlock();
+        // The inverter's output pin may be legitimately unconnected: CTS's
+        // own characterization phase (configure_cts_characterization)
+        // places dummy "<dummyload_prefix_>NNN" instances (see
+        // CtsOptions::getDummyLoadPrefix) on real clock nets to measure
+        // delay/slew under representative fanout, and those instances'
+        // outputs are deliberately left floating. Treating every Liberty
+        // inverter found on a net as a real pass-through stage with a
+        // valid downstream net null-derefs on exactly that case.
+        odb::dbITerm* outputTerm = inst->getFirstOutput();
+        odb::dbNet* outputNet = outputTerm ? outputTerm->getNet() : nullptr;
+        if (outputNet) {
+          odb::dbITerm* invertedTerm = outputNet->get1stSignalInput(false);
+          if (invertedTerm) {
+            nonSinkMacro &= invertedTerm->getInst()->isBlock();
+          }
+        }
       }
 
       if (hasInsertionDelay(inst, mterm) || nonSinkMacro || inst->isBlock()) {
-- 
